":"online resource"}],"extent":"1 online resource (127 p.)"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"In this classic argument for curriculum reform in early education, Jerome Bruner shows that the basic concepts of science and the humanities can be grasped intuitively at a very early age. He argues persuasively that curricula should he designed to foster such early intuitions and then build on them in increasingly formal and abstract ways as education progresses. Bruner's foundational case for the spiral curriculum has influenced a generation of educators and will continue to be a source of insight into the goals and methods of the educational process"},"tableOfContents":"Frontmatter --  PREFACE, 1977 --  PREFACE --  MEMBERS OF THE WOODS HOLE CONFERENCE --  CONTENTS --  1 INTRODUCTION --  2 THE IMPORTANCE OF STRUCTURE --  3 READINESS FOR LEARNING --  4 INTUITIVE AND ANALYTIC THINKING --  5 MOTIVES FOR LEARNING --  6 AIDS TO TEACHING --  INDEX","targetAudience":{"@authority":"marctarget","#text":"specialized"},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Jerome Bruner"},{"@type":"system details","#text":"Mode of access: Internet via World Wide Web"},{"@type":"language","#text":"In English"}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Education -- Aims and objectives"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Electronic books. -- local"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"EDUCATION / General"}],"classification":{"@authority":"ddc","#text":"370.1"},"relatedItem":[{"@type":"host","@otherType":"Title is part of eBook package:","@displayLabel":"Title is part of eBook package:","titleInfo":{"title":"HUP eBook Package Archive 1893-1999"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"De Gruyter"}},null,{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99158718774503941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780674028999"},{"@type":"doi","#text":"10.4159/9780674028999"}],"location":[null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"originalDocument":"https://s3.am